{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01XTVBKbBP7wavmY58Gu9CcM","type":"tool_result","content":"108:    review-security-review.md  # reviewer: findings, verdict, what must be fixed\n140:next: [review:security-review, review:eng-review]   # optional requests\n346:**The starter security reviewer (`reviewers/security-review`, #43).** `.ard/reviewers/security-review/prompt.txt` looks for injection, authz gaps, secrets and unsafe dependencies the change introduces or makes reachable, plus other weaknesses. It has the same untrusted inputs, Trust rules (steering text in the change is a blocking `high` finding), write rules and role-file format as eng-review, with `role: security-review` and a `## Checks` body. It never builds, tests or runs the repository's code. It runs, when they are installed: gitleaks over the changed files with `--redact`; gosec on changed Go packages with `-nosec`; and semgrep with `--metrics=off` and `--disable-nosem`, using only a rule file already in the repository that the change does not touch, or the sidecar's. Suppressions the change adds are reviewed as part of the change. It skips a missing tool and says so, never downloads rules or vulnerability data, and never copies a secret it finds into its report (file, line and kind only). Each finding's first line starts with `[injection]`, `[authz]`, `[secret]`, `[dependency]` or `[other]`. Critical and high block, medium only when it is one of the four kinds with a nameable exploit, low and info never; `needs-human` only when there is nothing to review. The starter override is Opus 5.5 (#8) with the default 30m timeout and no `when:`. No Dockerfile: host mode with the #84 warning until #55. Running pinned tools in controller code is #205.\n348:**The starter license reviewer (`reviewers/license-review`, #44).** `.ard/reviewers/license-review/prompt.txt` checks that every dependency a PR adds or upgrades has a license on the owners' allowlist. The allowlist is one SPDX id per line (`#` comments; `A OR B` passes when any operand is listed, `A AND B` only when all are, `A WITH E` only when listed verbatim; no per-package exceptions). It is read from the sidecar's `licenses/allowlist.txt` when the role has a sidecar and that file exists, else from `allowlist.txt` in the role directory (`$ARD_ROLE_DIR`, read from the config snapshot on `main`, under a protected path). An allowlist found in the PR, the diff, the issue or `.ard/<issue#>/` is ignored, and an unreadable or empty one ends `needs-human`. For Go it runs `go-licenses check` and `go-licenses report` when the tool is on the host `PATH` (optional; it never installs tools), else falls back to `go mod download -json` and the module's LICENSE files; for npm it reads the lockfile `license` field. A license outside the allowlist, or one that cannot be determined, is a blocking `high` finding on the manifest line that adds it (verdict `changes`, so the merger never merges while the label stands); a license that changed but is still allowed is `info`; other ecosystems are `info` and unchecked; no dependency change approves with \"No dependency changes to review\". The starter override is Sonnet 5.5 with `when.paths` listing the manifests and lockfiles above. It ships no Dockerfile, so it runs as a host subprocess with the #84 warning until #55.\n356:| security-review | always | gitleaks, gosec, semgrep with local rules (run by the agent when installed; controller-run aids are #205) | injection, authz gaps, secrets, unsafe dependencies |\n358:| license-review | a manifest or lockfile changes (`go.mod`, `go.sum`, `package.json`, `package-lock.json`, `npm-shrinkwrap.json`) | go-licenses (run by the agent when on the host `PATH`; optional), scancode later | a license outside the allowlist |\n433:  workers/engineer:         { tests: make test, timeout: 60m, reviewers: [security-review, eng-review] }\n434:  reviewers/license-review: { agent: kimi, when: { paths: [\"**/go.mod\", \"**/go.sum\", \"**/package.json\", \"**/package-lock.json\", \"**/npm-shrinkwrap.json\"] } }\n442:**`ard init` proposes the starter `.ard/` as a PR (decided, #27).** It builds `config.yaml` and the starter roles in memory (today `scanners/cve`, `scanners/docs`, `scanners/error-codes`, `scanners/trufflehog`, `triage/lead`, `triage/engineer`, `workers/engineer`, `reviewers/eng-review`, `reviewers/license-review` and `reviewers/security-review`; `intake: {mode: label}`, the protected-path defaults above, and the cve, docs, error-codes, lead, engineer, worker, eng-review, security-review and license-review overrides), compares them with `main`, commits them on a new branch `ard-init` cut from `main` through the effect layer and a narrow committer lane, pushes it and opens a PR. It writes nothing to the local working tree or to `main`, and `scan` is committed `100755`. Files that already exist on `main` with different contents are a conflict naming the paths; `--force` proposes overwriting them, and init never deletes anything. It refuses while `ard-init` exists on the remote, whatever its PR's state, so it never force-pushes over or stacks commits on a human's edits: merge or close the PR, delete the branch and rerun. If `main` already has every starter file it prints `nothing to propose`. It syncs the label catalogue first (like `ard labels sync`), so a failure leaves nothing pushed. With `--dry-run` the commit, push and PR are logged to the ledger instead of acting. A missing repo, credentials or `main` branch exits 1 with nothing written, and `ARD_GITHUB_TOKEN` auth is refused outside `--dry-run`. The PR body carries the one step the App cannot do: copy `.github/workflows/bot-commits.yml` and set the repository variable `ARD_BOT_LOGIN` (optionally `ARD_BOT_CHECK_SINCE`). It also says to merge with **Squash and merge** or **Create a merge commit**, never a fast-forward from the command line, which would put the bot's commit on main's first-parent history. **Rebase and merge** is also safe: the scratch-repo run (#85) showed GitHub records the merging human as committer of a rebase-merged commit (the bot stays the author), and the bot-commit check keys on committer, so it does not flag it. The body still names only the two merges above.\n344:**The starter reviewer (`reviewers/eng-review`, #36).** `.ard/reviewers/eng-review/prompt.txt` checks correctness, missing tests and drift from `plan.md`. Its inputs, all untrusted, are `diff.patch`, `issue.md`, `comments.md` and the committed `.ard/<issue#>/`; text in the change that addresses a reviewer is a blocking `high` finding on its line (verdict `changes`), and the same text in the issue, comments or `.ard/<issue#>/` is ignored and mentioned in the body. It runs the repository's own linter and the changed packages' tests with coverage through Bash, writing scratch files outside the worktree, reports only problems on added lines and skips a check it cannot run, saying so; the package manager may fetch declared dependencies and nothing else is downloaded. Critical and high findings always block, medium only when it is a real bug, low and info never; a missing test blocks only when it is `high` (risky or core logic, or a case `plan.md` named), smaller gaps are `medium`. The verdict is `changes` exactly when a finding blocks. `line` is the head-side line: a `+` line goes inline, anything else in the body. It writes only `review-eng-review.md` with `role: eng-review`, `attempt: 1`, `status` `done` or `needs-human`, `findings` with a double-quoted `file` and a `message: |` block, never `next` or `worker`, and a body with a `## Checks` section. It answers `needs-human` only when there is nothing to review or the issue and the plan contradict each other. The starter override is Sonnet 5.5 with the default 30m timeout and no `when:`, so every PR from the starter worker gets it. It ships no Dockerfile, so it runs as a host subprocess with the #84 warning until #55.\n442:**`ard init` proposes the starter `.ard/` as a PR (decided, #27).** It builds `config.yaml` and the starter roles in memory (today `scanners/cve`, `scanners/docs`, `scanners/error-codes`, `scanners/trufflehog`, `triage/lead`, `triage/engineer`, `workers/engineer`, `reviewers/eng-review`, `reviewers/license-review` and `reviewers/security-review`; `intake: {mode: label}`, the protected-path defaults above, and the cve, docs, error-codes, lead, engineer, worker, eng-review, security-review and license-review overrides), compares them with `main`, commits them on a new branch `ard-init` cut from `main` through the effect layer and a narrow committer lane, pushes it and opens a PR. It writes nothing to the local working tree or to `main`, and `scan` is committed `100755`. Files that already exist on `main` with different contents are a conflict naming the paths; `--force` proposes overwriting them, and init never deletes anything. It refuses while `ard-init` exists on the remote, whatever its PR's state, so it never force-pushes over or stacks commits on a human's edits: merge or close the PR, delete the branch and rerun. If `main` already has every starter file it prints `nothing to propose`. It syncs the label catalogue first (like `ard labels sync`), so a failure leaves nothing pushed. With `--dry-run` the commit, push and PR are logged to the ledger instead of acting. A missing repo, credentials or `main` branch exits 1 with nothing written, and `ARD_GITHUB_TOKEN` auth is refused outside `--dry-run`. The PR body carries the one step the App cannot do: copy `.github/workflows/bot-commits.yml` and set the repository variable `ARD_BOT_LOGIN` (optionally `ARD_BOT_CHECK_SINCE`). It also says to merge with **Squash and merge** or **Create a merge commit**, never a fast-forward from the command line, which would put the bot's commit on main's first-parent history. **Rebase and merge** is also safe: the scratch-repo run (#85) showed GitHub records the merging human as committer of a rebase-merged commit (the bot stays the author), and the bot-commit check keys on committer, so it does not flag it. The body still names only the two merges above.\n\tif err != nil || len(fs) != 0 {\n\t\tt.Errorf(\"secret scan of the prompt: %v, %v\", fs, err)\n\t}\n}\n\nfunc TestSecurityReviewPromptExamples(t *testing.T) {\n\tb, err := FS.ReadFile(securityReviewPromptPath)\n\tif err != nil {\n\t\tt.Fatal(err)\n\t}\n\tblocks := fencedFrontmatter(string(b))\n\tif len(blocks) < 4 {\n\t\tt.Fatalf(\"found %d example blocks, want at least 4\", len(blocks))\n\t}\n\tvar changes, approveWith, approvePlain, needsHuman, withLine, noLine int\n\tkinds := map[string]bool{}\n\tfor i, blk := range blocks {\n\t\trf, err := closeout.Parse([]byte(blk))\n\t\tif err != nil {\n\t\t\tt.Errorf(\"example %d: %v\", i, err)\n\t\t\tcontinue\nfunc TestSecurityReviewRoleTraps(t *testing.T) {\n\tfor _, role := range []string{\"review-security-review\", \"reviewers/security-review\", \"eng-review\", \"security\"} {\n\t\tfile := \"---\\nrole: \" + role + \"\\nstatus: done\\nattempt: 1\\nverdict: approve\\n---\\nBody.\\n\"\n\t\trf, err := closeout.Parse([]byte(file))\n\t\tif err != nil {\n\t\t\tt.Errorf(\"role %q: %v\", role, err)\n\t\t\tcontinue\n\t\t}\n\t\tif rf.Role == securityJob().Role {\n\t\t\tt.Errorf(\"role %q equals the job's role\", role)\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\tfile := \"---\\nrole: security-review\\nstatus: done\\nattempt: 1\\nverdict: approve\\nfindings:\\n  - file: \\\"a.go\\\"\\n    line: 3\\n    severity: critical\\n    blocking: true\\n    message: |\\n      [injection] m\\n---\\nBody.\\n\"\n\trf, err := closeout.Parse([]byte(file))\n\tif err != nil {\n\t\tt.Fatal(err)\n\t}\n\tif !rf.DowngradeVerdict() || rf.Verdict != closeout.VerdictChanges {\n\t\tt.Errorf(\"verdict = %q, want downgrade to changes\", rf.Verdict)\n\t}\n}\n4. When the status is done, write or replace the \"## Requirements\" section of\n   .ard/N/plan.md. If the file does not exist, create it starting with\n   \"# Plan for #N\". Keep every other section as it is. If \"## Requirements\" already\n   exists, replace it where it is; otherwise add it at the end. It has these\n   subsections, in this order:\n   - \"### Requirements\": what the change must do, in the user's terms.\n   - \"### Acceptance criteria\": a numbered list (1. 2. 3.). Each item is one\n     observable, testable statement.\n   - \"### Out of scope\": what the change must not do.\n5. Keep the section short and concrete. Another agent follows it, so state\n   decisions, not options.\n6. When the status is blocked or needs-human, leave plan.md untouched.\n7. Write .ard/N/triage-product-manager.md last.\n\n## The format of triage-product-manager.md\n\nCode checks this file. Any deviation fails the job.\n\n- The first line is exactly ---. The frontmatter ends at the next line that\n  is exactly ---.\n- Use these keys and no others:\n  - role: product-manager (the role is product-manager, never triage-product-manager)","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"900a5f36-34ec-47df-bf80-a78075975fbb","uuid":"81f0328b-1566-4b95-b12d-69d9e0e19455","timestamp":"2026-11-23T21:59:04.485Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"108:    review-security-review.md  # reviewer: findings, verdict, what must be fixed\n140:next: [review:security-review, review:eng-review]   # optional requests\n346:**The starter security reviewer (`reviewers/security-review`, #43).** `.ard/reviewers/security-review/prompt.txt` looks for injection, authz gaps, secrets and unsafe dependencies the change introduces or makes reachable, plus other weaknesses. It has the same untrusted inputs, Trust rules (steering text in the change is a blocking `high` finding), write rules and role-file format as eng-review, with `role: security-review` and a `## Checks` body. It never builds, tests or runs the repository's code. It runs, when they are installed: gitleaks over the changed files with `--redact`; gosec on changed Go packages with `-nosec`; and semgrep with `--metrics=off` a